PARS = [
("Decision analysis evaluates a given set", """Under time pressure, decision analysis, which evaluates a given set of alternatives {35;36}, is a weak point. Option generation precedes choice yet remains a neglected phase {18}, even though it can be formalized as a recursion of decision problems {19;37}, and systems that hard-wire the open parts of a problem are not adopted {20}. Cognitive evidence agrees, since generating options in ill-structured problems requires ideation beyond memory retrieval and raises the quality of the best option {38}, and operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation and filter them by a strict, external evaluation."""),
("Mechanisms that generate alternatives", """Mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern. Some search a fixed model for structurally different solutions {21;22}. Some combine modules under compatibility constraints {17;39}. Some add new actions to the action model at run time {40;41}. In every case the new alternatives are built from parts the system already understands. In other words, an open decision space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. Mechanisms that change a rule base at run time follow the same pattern. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the vocabulary {33;34}. Value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {42}. Generative models draft rules outside the current structure {43}. VODA uses all three, with tuning and selection in the closed layer and generation in the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {44} that keeps generation cheap to reject and the evaluator independent of the generator."""),
]
